---
title: "Scripts and errors"
description: "Write strict QQL scripts and handle structured parse, validation, plan, and execution errors."
sidebar:
  order: 7
---

## Script rules

Statements are separated by one semicolon. A final semicolon is optional.
Leading separators, repeated separators, adjacent unseparated statements, and
more than 256 statements are rejected.

{% qqlExample title="Provision and query" %}
CREATE COLLECTION catalog (
  dense VECTOR(384, COSINE)
);
CREATE INDEX ON COLLECTION catalog FOR category TYPE keyword;
UPSERT INTO catalog VALUES {id: 1, text: 'red chair', category: 'furniture'};
QUERY TEXT 'chair' FROM catalog USING dense LIMIT 5;
{% /qqlExample %}

## Parse APIs

| API | Contract |
|---|---|
| `Parser::parse` | Exactly one complete statement |
| `Parser::parse_all` | One complete semicolon-delimited script |
| SDK `parse` | Host-friendly wrapper returning statement objects |
| Executor `execute` | One statement or script; chooses the shared path |
| Host array input | Independent inputs, each parsed separately |

## Structured failures

QQL errors carry a stable code, error kind, message, and optional zero-based
UTF-8 byte span. The main families are parse, validation, planning, embedding,
transport, backend, and batch-cardinality errors.

{% aside type="tip" title="Validate the real operation" %}
Do not add a second parse pass just to “validate” before parsing again. Parse
once, keep the typed statement, apply policy, then plan or execute it.
{% /aside %}
